Capacitors $C_1$ and $C_2$ are in parallel, their equivalent capacitance is in series with capacitor $C_3$. Hence, the equivalent capacitance of the given circuit is


$$
C_{e q}=\frac{\left(C_1+C_2\right) C_3}{C_1+C_2+C_3}
$$
Charge on capacitor $C_3$ is
$$
\begin{aligned}
Q & =V C_{\mathrm{eq}} \\
& =\frac{V\left(C_1+C_2\right) C_3}{C_1+C_2+C_3}
\end{aligned}
$$
Voltage across $C_3$ is
$$
=\frac{Q}{C_3}=\frac{V\left(C_1+C_2\right)}{C_1+C_2+C_3}
$$

Between the points $P$ and $Q$, a capacitor $C_1$ and series combination of $C_2, C_3, C_4, C_5$ are connected in parallel.
$\therefore \frac{1}{C_s}=\frac{1}{C_2}+\frac{1}{C_3}+\frac{1}{C_4}+\frac{1}{C_5}$
